{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by the wesleyan alumnus aaron sorkin. the film is based on the true story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the resulting lawsuits.\n\nthe film begins with a narrative by the character mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, as he recounts the events that led to the creation of facebook. the story is told through a series of flashbacks, showcasing the early days of facebook and the relationships between mark, his college roommate edward saverin (andrew garfield), and their fellow harvard students.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its portrayal of the complex and often fraught relationships between the main characters. the chemistry between the cast members is undeniable, with jesse eisenberg delivering a particularly impressive performance as the reclusive and socially awkward mark zuckerberg. andrew garfield shines as the charismatic and ambitious edward saverin, who becomes increasingly disillusioned with mark's growing success.\n\nthe film also explores the darker side of thefacebook phenomenon, including the ways in which it has changed the way we interact with each other and the world around us. the character of sean parker (joshua jackson) is particularly notable in this regard, as he serves as a sort of conscience for the film's protagonist, warning him of the potential consequences of his actions.\n\nfrom a technical standpoint, the film is a masterpiece. the cinematography is stunning, with a muted color palette that perfectly captures the bleak and sterile atmosphere of the harvard campus. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and evocative, perfectly complementing the film's themes of isolation and disconnection.\n\none of the most impressive aspects of the film is its script, which is written 